Subject: Population size using region.N in a smaller area than the mask
Message-ID: <OkGRSyFUajE.PRUOMmskAQAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/secrgroup/c/OkGRSyFUajE/m/PRUOMmskAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

We are conducting secr analyses to estimate lynx abundances and densities using camera trap surveys.
We would like to have opinions on estimating the population density with the function region.N on a smaller area than the area that was used in the model.
I'll explain with an example:
Let's say we have the following model:
Mbk <-secr.fit(lynx_capthist, mask=mask_prox, model = list(D~1, g0~bk, sigma~1))
Normally, we would conduct the region.N function taking the mask_prox (--> grey mask in the figure) used in the Mbk model: region.N(Mbk, region = mask_prox)
But we would like to obtain the population size only in a specific smaller part of the area (--> blue delimitation in the figure, in red are the sites of the camera traps) and not in the whole mask, but still using the same model: region.N(Mbk, region= blue_area)

So the question is: Is it possible to calculate a population size from a fitted secr model but on a smaller region than the one used in this fitted model?
If yes, is it still the case if we add a covariate to the density and/or a habitat suitability mask ?

Thanks,

Inès

From u118cea91a1619106 at groups.google.com  Wed Feb  1 22:25:51 2023
From: u118cea91a1619106 at groups.google.com (Murray Efford)
