Message-ID: <OkGRSyFUajE.SkXmsZ00AQAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <OkGRSyFUajE.PRUOMmskAQAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
References: <OkGRSyFUajE.PRUOMmskAQAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/secrgroup/c/OkGRSyFUajE/m/SkXmsZ00AQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Inès
There should be no problem in the current version of secr (a bug was fixed in October 2022). Covariates used in the density model should also appear as covariates of the new mask object. The simplest approach is to use a subset of the original mask, e.g.
newmask <- subset(oldmask, pointsInPolygon(oldmask, blue_area))
It may not be so easy if you provide a polygon for the 'region' argument of region.N. Looking at the code, the mask constructed internally from a region polygon doesn't seem to allow for covariates. So I would advise specifying 'region' as a mask if you want to use covariates. You can construct a new mask with closer spacing if you like.
Murray

